{"type": "answer", "run_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-key1b-20261111", "scenario": "eval:retrieval", "source": "real", "setting": "small_lean", "setting_def": {"piece_chars": 600, "k": 4, "rerank": false, "router": false}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "qid": "Q15", "request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-key1b-20261111-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q15", "start_time": "2026-11-11T07:24:17.085+00:00", "degraded": false, "reply": {"request_id": "retrieval-part2-lean-rest-key1b-20261111-small_lean-gpt-oss-120b-Q15", "question": "Why the ratio loss, and what are F, G and N?", "kind": "answer", "level": 0, "text": "The ratio loss is introduced to prevent the model from collapsing to trivial solutions that either keep almost every vector (defeating any speed‑up) or over‑compress the sequence and discard essential information; it acts as a regularizer that steers the network toward a desired compression level, much like load‑balancing losses used in Mixture‑of‑Experts models [S2].  It provides a differentiable objective that can be optimized even though the actual fraction of kept vectors is nondifferentiable, allowing the routing module to receive continuous feedback about how well the current compression matches the target [S4].  In the loss formulation, **F** denotes the empirical fraction of vectors that are actually selected (i.e., the proportion of boundaries that become hard selections) [S4], **G** is the average boundary probability predicted by the model (the mean of the soft decisions) [S4], and **N** is the hyper‑parameter that sets the target compression ratio (the desired proportion of vectors to retain) [S4].  The loss reaches its minimum value of 1 when the model’s selection fraction matches the target (F = G = 1/N) and, in practice, encourages F to converge toward G so that the compression ratio aligns with N [S1][S2].", "sources": [{"sid": "S1", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0056", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b019"]}, {"sid": "S2", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0054", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b006", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b007", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b008", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b009", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b010", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b011", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b012", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b013", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b014", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b015", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b016", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b017"]}, {"sid": "S3", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0057", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b020", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b021", "dynamic_chunking-p08-b022"]}, {"sid": "S4", "cite": "2507.07955v2 p.8 §2.2.3", "pid": "dynamic_chunking:600:0055", "paper": "dynamic_chunking", "block_ids": ["dynamic_chunking-p08-b018"]}], "notice": "checked for citations only (no second-model check)", "eta_s": null, "model_calls": [{"step": "draft", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "status": 200, "outcome": "ok", "prompt_tokens": 860, "completion_tokens": 294, "tokens_estimated": false, "ms": 1162.9102000006242, "waited_s": 0.0}], "spans": [{"name": "plan", "start_ms": 0.0, "end_ms": 0.8, "model": null, "outcome": "full", "detail": {"predicted_wait_s": {"full": {"openai/gpt-oss-120b": 0.0}}, "drafter": "openai/gpt-oss-120b"}}, {"name": "understand", "start_ms": 0.8, "end_ms": 0.8, "model": null, "outcome": "off (config)", "detail": {}}, {"name": "retrieve", "start_ms": 0.8, "end_ms": 1.6, "model": null, "outcome": "1 search", "detail": {"query": "Why the ratio loss, and what are F, G and N?", "papers": null, "k": 4, "evidence": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 23.515, "query_terms": 5}}}, {"name": "decide", "start_ms": 1.6, "end_ms": 1.6, "model": null, "outcome": "draft", "detail": {"best": 1.0, "union": 1.0, "top_bm25": 23.515, "query_terms": 5}}, {"name": "draft", "start_ms": 1.6, "end_ms": 1166.9, "model": null, "outc